{
  "description": "Test vectors for nexus-client's e2e module (src/e2e.rs), for every implementation to check against: the Rust client core and the browser's WebCrypto side. Made with Node's crypto, independently of the Rust code. Byte fields are hex. A length-prefixed list is each field's byte length as 2 big-endian bytes, then its bytes; epochs and seqs are 8 big-endian bytes, ids UTF-8. An event's aad is the length-prefixed labels.event_aad, thread_id, epoch, seq; sealed is nonce || AES-256-GCM ciphertext || tag. A lock is HPKE (RFC 9180) base mode with info the length-prefixed labels.lock_info, thread_id, epoch, holder_id, and an empty aad; locked is enc || ciphertext || tag; ikm_e is the ephemeral key's input to DeriveKeyPair, as in RFC 9180's own test vectors. A device's secret is its X25519 private key || its Ed25519 seed. A fingerprint is the SHA-256 of the length-prefixed labels.fingerprint, X25519 key, Ed25519 key (empty for a recovery key); short_code is its first 10 bytes in RFC 4648 base32. Signatures are Ed25519 by signer_secret's seed over message, the text Gateway verifies; keys and locked copies in a message are base64url without padding, and key_version is epoch + 1. Recovery keys: a recovery code is 16 random bytes shown as 26 Crockford base32 digits (most significant first) and Crockford's mod-37 check symbol, grouped 5-5-5-4-4-4 with hyphens; its holder's X25519 private key (secret) is HKDF-SHA256 with ikm the 16 bytes, salt salts.recovery_code and info the length-prefixed person_id. recovery_code_typos must all be refused. A passkey's PRF input (eval.first) is the SHA-256 of passkey_prf_label; its holder's private key is HKDF-SHA256 with ikm the 32-byte PRF output, salt salts.passkey and info the length-prefixed person_id and credential_id bytes. holder_approvals are signer_secret's signatures over nexus-device-approval:v2:{holder_id}:{kind}:{encryption key in base64url}: with an empty Ed25519 field. A recovery challenge is 32 random bytes Gateway seals to one trusted recovery key, one challenge per key: HPKE base mode as a lock, with info the length-prefixed labels.recover_challenge, person_id, holder_id, and an empty aad; sealed is enc || ciphertext || tag. A handover is an approver's trusted list for the device it approves: HPKE base mode as a lock, with info the length-prefixed labels.handover, device_id, approver_id, and an empty aad; the plaintext is signer_secret's Ed25519 signature over message (nexus-device-handover:v1:{device_id}:{approver_id}:{payload in base64url}), then payload; sealed is enc || ciphertext || tag, sealed to the X25519 key of devices[device_index]. Unlike the rest, each was sealed by one of the two implementations (sealed_by), with a random ephemeral key, so both open what the other sealed.",
